# `case _: Map[?, ?]`

`BUGS.md` `type-ascription-tuple-and-set-arms-missing`. The JS lane's type-test table had no `Map`
arm, so `case _: Map[?, ?]` fell through and answered `other` where INT, the native lane and the JVM
all answered `map`.

A Map **is** distinguishable on that lane — the runtime already carries `_isMap`
(`x instanceof Map || x instanceof _HAMT`), and it ships unconditionally. So this was a table gap,
not a representation gap, which is why it could be closed while `Set` and `List` could not: measured
2026-07-30, `List(...)` is `[...args]` and `_setOf(...)` returns a plain array too, so on that lane
those two are the SAME value and any arm would have to lie about one of them.

```scalascript
def kind(x: Any): String = x match
  case _: Map[?, ?] => "map"
  case _ => "other"

println(kind(Map("a" -> 1)))
println(kind(Map()))
println(kind(List(1, 2)))
println(kind("s"))
println(kind(7))
```

The empty Map is the case a `size > 0` style test would get wrong, and the three non-Maps are the
guard that the new arm did not start swallowing everything — a container, a String and a number, all
of which must still reach the wildcard.
